#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# cone

#% text
# `cone([[x1, y1, z1], [x2, y2, z2]], r)` is a cone with a base of radius r at the first point and its apex at the second, in `graphics3d`.

#% text
# `cone()` stands on the unit disk at z = -1 with its apex at z = 1.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Coordinates are in the units of the graphic; the box around the scene follows the data unless `plot_range` says otherwise.

#% item
# Colours, `opacity` and `edge_form` before it in its list style it; faces are lit.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The default:

#% cell
graphics3d(cone())
#% output
#= image("cone.files/cone-1.svgz")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A wide and a narrow cone:

#% cell
graphics3d([cone([[0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 1]], 1), red, cone([[3, 0, 0], [3, 0, 2]], 0.3)])
#% output
#= image("cone.files/cone-2.svgz")

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A cone pointing sideways, with axes:

#% cell
graphics3d(cone([[0, 0, 0], [2, 0, 0]], 0.5), axes: true)
#% output
#= image("cone.files/cone-3.svgz")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `graphics3d`, `sphere`, `plot3d`
